• I’ll inspect the review flow, its existing fake-reviewer controls, and the issue/documentation formats before making the fix.

• The current verifier is the single provenance gate for both `train.sh` and `signoff.sh`, so the new key can be enforced there without changing landing policy. I’m checking all clean-note fixtures next, because legacy diff-only notes must now fail deliberately rather than break unrelated controls.

• The implementation will send each diff piece inside the saved prompt (so Codex’s stdin can stay closed), extract `HEAD` into a temporary archive, and fail closed on either missing readability evidence or known sandbox errors. I’ll also make old clean notes re-reviewable instead of letting `review.sh` short-circuit on them.

• The reviewer now runs against an archived `HEAD`, receives the diff in a persisted prompt, and can only produce a new clean note after explicit repository-readable evidence. Next I’m updating the verifier and the fake-reviewer controls, including all three blocked-access cases.

• The verifier now rejects legacy clean notes with exit 1 and an explicit `repository: readable` message. I’m wiring the controls to prove the archived tree is used, stdin is empty, prompts round-trip byte for byte, and unreadable/sandbox/missing-marker replies all become blocked.

• The controls are in place. I’m running the review-tool gate now to catch interface or parsing mistakes before updating the landing fixtures and documentation.

• The first control run passed the new safety cases; four older assertions failed only because the fake reviewer’s log gained fields after `files=`. I’ve restored that log’s original suffix and am rerunning the gate.

• All 115 review-tool controls now pass. I’m documenting the new note contract and archived-reviewer behavior, filing both issues as closed by this branch, and updating the landing/sign-off comments so their exit-status assumptions stay explicit.

• The issue rows and documentation are written. I’m doing a final consistency pass now; the full root check set is build-free in this tree, so I’ll run it after the targeted landing and issue checks.

• The documentation and issue ledger are complete. I’m running the landing-script and issue checks in parallel, then I’ll run the full build-free root gate set and inspect the final diff before committing.

• The full root run passed 10 of 11 checks, including all changed areas, but `landing_ready.sh` failed one bisect CI-call-count assertion (`bisect --ci runs: ''`). I’m rerunning that gate alone to determine whether it is a reproducible integration issue or an unrelated flaky control.

• The same `landing_ready.sh` failure reproduces alone. Since this branch changes no bisect or CI-dispatch logic, I’m checking the pristine base from a temporary `git archive`; that will distinguish a pre-existing red gate from a regression before I commit.

• The base comparison isolated the cause: `landing_ready.sh` creates clean notes directly, and its bisect path now correctly refuses those legacy notes before dispatching CI. I’m updating that fixture to include `repository: readable`; the production bisect logic needs no change.

• The readiness fixture now passes all 8 controls, including two distinct bisect CI dispatches. I’m rerunning the complete root set on the corrected deliverable.

• All 11 root checks now pass. I’m committing the reviewed file set on `wip/review-access` without pushing, then I’ll rerun the root checks once against the committed SHA so the final result is bound to the deliverable.

• Implemented and committed on `wip/review-access` as `dc3d6a802` (`ISS-0021 ISS-0022: require readable review provenance`). Nothing was pushed; the worktree is clean.

  - `scripts/review/review.sh` now runs Codex with `danger-full-access` in a temporary `git archive` of the reviewed commit, with stdin closed and each diff piece included in the prompt.
  - Every prompt is saved as `.review/<sha>-<round>.prompt.<invocation>.txt`, with its sha256 recorded in the raw output.
  - Missing/unreadable repository markers and known sandbox failures produce `blocked`, never clean.
  - Clean notes now append `repository: readable`. `scripts/review/verify.sh` rejects clean notes lacking it with exit 1 and a message naming the missing key.
  - Legacy clean notes no longer short-circuit `review.sh`; the commit is reviewed again.
  - ISS-0021 and ISS-0022 are filed closed in `ISSUES.tsv`, including Codex’s confirmation and the 42-of-59 evidence.

  Files changed: `AGENTS.md`, `ISSUES.tsv`, `docs/REVIEW.md`, `docs/LANDING.md`, `changelog.d/2026-11-08-review-access.md`, both review scripts, review/landing controls, and the note-format comments in `scripts/land/{notes,signoff,train}.sh`.

  Checks inside `nix develop .#gates`:

